/*
 * LC 128 — Longest Consecutive Sequence
 *
 * PROBLEM:
 * Given unsorted integer array nums, return length of longest
 * consecutive elements sequence.
 * Must run in O(n) time.
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS:
 * - 0 <= nums.length <= 10^5
 * - -10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9
 * - Array unsorted — can't use sorting (O(n log n))
 * - Duplicates possible — HashSet naturally deduplicates
 * - Must be O(n)
 *
 * CORE INSIGHT — SEQUENCE START DETECTION:
 * Only start counting from the ACTUAL start of a sequence.
 * num is a sequence start if (num-1) NOT in set.
 * Skipping non-starts is critical for O(n):
 *   Without skip: every element triggers chain check → O(n²)
 *   With skip:    each element visited at most twice → O(n)
 *
 * WHY O(n) NOT O(n+k):
 * Each number visited at most twice:
 *   Once in outer for loop
 *   At most once inside a while loop
 * Sum of ALL while loop iterations across ALL sequence starts = n
 * Each element belongs to exactly one sequence → total = 2n → O(n)
 *
 * WHY HashSet not sorting:
 * Sorting → O(n log n) — violates O(n) constraint
 * HashSet → O(1) contains() → enables O(n) solution
 *
 * WHY iterate set not nums:
 * nums may have duplicates → same num checked twice unnecessarily
 * set has unique elements → each num checked exactly once
 *
 * ALL APPROACHES:
 * ┌──────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┬──────────┬──────┐
 * │ Approach         │ Idea                             │ TC       │ SC   │
 * ├──────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼──────────┼──────┤
 * │ Brute Force      │ Sort + scan adjacent             │ O(nlogn) │ O(1) │
 * │ HashSet          │ Set + sequence start detection   │ O(n)     │ O(n) │
 * └──────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┴──────────┴──────┘
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 1 — BRUTE FORCE (Sorting)
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY: Sort array → consecutive elements become adjacent.
 * HOW: Sort → scan → if nums[i]+1 == nums[i+1] → extend sequence
 *      Handle duplicates — skip if nums[i] == nums[i+1].
 *
 * TC: O(n log n) — sorting dominates
 * SC: O(1)       — in-place sort
 * REJECTED: Violates O(n) requirement
 */
public int longestConsecutiveBrute(int[] nums) {
    if(nums.length == 0) return 0;
    Arrays.sort(nums);
    int maxLength = 1, length = 1;
    for(int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++){
        if(nums[i] == nums[i-1]) continue; // skip duplicates
        if(nums[i] == nums[i-1] + 1) length++;
        else length = 1;
        maxLength = Math.max(maxLength, length);
    }
    return maxLength;
}

/*
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 2 — HASHSET (Optimal)
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY HashSet:
 * Need O(1) lookup — "does next consecutive number exist?"
 * HashSet.contains() → O(1) average.
 *
 * HOW:
 * Step 1 — add all nums to HashSet (deduplicates automatically)
 * Step 2 — iterate set:
 *   if num-1 NOT in set → num is sequence start
 *       count consecutive nums using while loop
 *       update max length
 *   if num-1 in set → skip (not a start)
 *
 * WHILE LOOP — two increments:
 * num++    → move to next consecutive number
 * length++ → increase sequence length
 * Both needed — without num++: infinite loop checking same number!
 *
 * Dry run — nums=[100,4,200,1,3,2]:
 * set = {100,4,200,1,3,2}
 * num=100: contains(99)?NO → start, while(101)?NO → length=1, result=1
 * num=4:   contains(3)?YES → skip
 * num=200: contains(199)?NO → start, while(201)?NO → length=1, result=1
 * num=1:   contains(0)?NO → start
 *          while(2)?YES → length=2,num=2
 *          while(3)?YES → length=3,num=3
 *          while(4)?YES → length=4,num=4
 *          while(5)?NO  → stop → result=4 ✅
 * num=3:   contains(2)?YES → skip
 * num=2:   contains(1)?YES → skip
 * return 4 ✅
 *
 * TC: O(n) — HashSet build O(n) + each element visited at most twice O(n)
 * SC: O(n) — HashSet stores all unique elements
 *
 * Edge cases:
 * - Empty array → result=0 → return 0 ✅
 * - All duplicates [1,1,1] → set={1} → length=1 ✅
 * - Single element → length=1 ✅
 * - Negative numbers → HashSet handles any Integer ✅
 */
public int longestConsecutive(int[] nums) {
    Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();
    for(int num : nums)
        set.add(num);
    int result = 0;
    for(int num : set){
        // only start counting from sequence start
        if(!set.contains(num - 1)){
            int length = 1;
            // count consecutive numbers
            while(set.contains(num + 1)){
                length++;
                num++;
            }
            result = Math.max(result, length);
        }
    }
    return result;
}
